WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.720
I hear from lots of
people every day

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:07.280
who are concerned about how
their diet is affecting their health.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.960
They need answers
based on facts.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:10.920
In other words,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.920
in the peer-reviewed
medical literature,

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:15.720
and that is what
I’m here for.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:19.000
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.600
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.920
Today we discover that
anatomy may not be destiny.

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.560
It turns out that in some countries 85%
of menopausal women suffer hot flashes.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:34.240
But in other countries that
number's only 15%.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:35.960
Here’s our first story.

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:39.080
SWAN, which stands for Study
of Women’s Health Across the Nation,

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.360
is by far the largest evaluation
of menopausal symptoms in history,

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:48.160
studying 16,000 American women
between the ages of 40 and 55.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.240
They found that women over
a body mass index of 27 or higher

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.240
were significantly more likely to suffer
from hot flashes or night sweats,

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.160
stiffness or soreness,
and urinary leakage.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.680
A BMI over 25 is considered overweight;
30 is obese;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.360
and the average U.S. BMI is 29.8.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.880
Since fatty tissue is a source for estrogen
one might expect the reverse,

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.400
but the link between excess body fat
and worse menopausal symptoms

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.720
indeed seems causal since women
randomized to a weight loss intervention

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:21.040
experience significant improvement
in hot flashes,

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:24.800
an effect that may extend
to bariatric surgery.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.360
In the SWAN study,
smoking and inactivity

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.840
were also associated
with more frequent symptoms.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.960
It’s always a good idea
to quit smoking and exercise,

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.600
but there are no interventional trials
on smoking cessation

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.600
and menopausal symptoms
to prove cause-and-effect,

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:43.160
and randomized controlled trials of exercise
failed to show any symptom relief.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:46.280
Of course you could just
take hormone therapy,

00:01:46.280 --> 00:01:49.560
but at the cost of significantly higher risk
of suffering a heart attack,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:53.360
stroke, blood clot, breast cancer,
or gallbladder disease.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.520
What can we do naturally to avoid
the extra clots and cancer?

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.840
Hot flashes, also referred to
as hot flushes,

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:04.320
are the most common menopausal symptom
for which women seek treatment.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:08.080
They afflict up to 80 to 85 percent
of European and American women,

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:12.200
lasting, along with night sweats,
for an average of more than seven years.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.040
But these symptoms are not universal.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.280
Though in North America
they may be considered inevitable,

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:23.960
if you noticed in Japan, for example,
only 15 percent may be affected.

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:28.680
In fact, there isn’t even a term
for hot flash in the Japanese language.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.760
The absence of even a word for it
in Japan is all the more remarkable

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.280
because Japanese is said to be
“infinitely more sensitive” than English

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:38.320
in describing bodily states,

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:42.440
with all sorts of extremely subtle
distinctions for somatic sensations.

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:46.840
For example, there are all sorts of specific
terms describing different sensations.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:50.480
In Japanese there are 20
or more words just to describe

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.080
the state of one's stomach
and intestines alone.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.200
But hot flashes appear
to be so unusual there,

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.160
researchers had to come up with ways
to describe it in Japanese surveys.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:05.320
Investigators suspected
it might be the soy.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:08.920
Dietary surveys have estimated
that soy isoflavone intake,

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.680
the phytoestrogens in soy,

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:13.760
is at least ten times higher
than in Europe or North America.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.560
And one comparison
of urine metabolites revealed

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:19.720
Japanese women have a hundred times
more phytoestrogens

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.240
flowing through their body
at any one time.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.440
To see if this might be playing a role,

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:27.120
researchers followed
a thousand Japanese women over time,

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:31.120
starting before they reached menopause,
to see if soy consumption was linked

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.080
to whether or not
they developed hot flashes.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.280
Women eating around four ounces
of tofu a day only had half the risk

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:40.960
compared to women only eating
an ounce or two a day.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.480
The researchers concluded, “Our findings
suggest that consumption of soy products

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:48.320
is a practical strategy
for preventing hot flashes.”

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.600
But only if interventional trials prove
they actually work,

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:56.190
which I’ll cover next.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:57.800
Is the consumption of soy

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.169
the reason why there isn’t even a word
for hot flash in Japanese?

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:05.440
Approximately 80% of perimenopausal
and postmenopausal women surveyed

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.960
choose to try natural means
to deal with symptoms,

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:12.640
and the single most common approach
was soy. But does it work?

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:15.720
Outside of an Asian context,
soy milk may be

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.320
a greater source of isoflavones
than soy foods,

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:21.160
and that seems ready-made
for a randomized trial

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.400
against dairy milk
or another plant milk control.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.400
Soy milk consumption has been associated
with fewer hot flashes and night sweats

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:31.240
and skim milk
with worse menopausal symptoms,

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:33.920
but when actually put to the test
against each other

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:36.520
there does not appear
to be any difference.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.280
There have been studies showing
that randomizing women to soy milk

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:41.720
can reduce hot flashes
and vaginal symptoms

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:44.240
as much as 70%
compared to the control group,

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.280
but without a placebo control,

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:49.240
we’re not sure how much of that
is the placebo effect.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:53.320
To truly blind study subjects,
soy isoflavones were extracted out

00:04:53.320 --> 00:04:55.080
and stuffed into capsules

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:59.120
to be pitted against identical looking
“sugar pill” placebo capsules.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:02.040
More than a dozen such clinical studies
have been performed—

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.520
randomized double-blind,
placebo-controlled trials

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:09.560
—and indeed, the equivalent of about
two servings of soy foods a day worth

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:13.520
reduced hot flash frequency
by about 20% more than placebo

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:18.240
and hot flash severity by around
25% more than placebo,

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:21.320
compared to more like&nbsp;
a 30 to 40% net reduction

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.840
from estrogen hormone therapy.

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:28.040
Soy isoflavones have also been shown
to improve other menopausal concerns

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:33.040
including vaginal dryness, bone loss,
memory and cognitive functions

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:36.040
more generally,
as well as depression.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:37.160
There have been a few trials

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.320
in which soy was pitted head-to-head
against hormone therapy.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:43.960
One found their efficacy comparable,
in terms of reducing hot flashes,

00:05:43.960 --> 00:05:46.557
muscle and joint pain,
and vaginal dryness.

00:05:46.557 --> 00:05:48.920
In the other, hormones worked
twice as well,

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:51.480
a 54% reduction in hot flash scores

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.560
taking the standard
estrogen/progesterone combination

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.480
compared to 24% over placebo
in the soy isoflavone group.

00:05:59.480 --> 00:06:03.320
Not only did the hormones work better,
but they can work quicker.

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:04.760
Three months are required

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:08.240
for soy isoflavones to achieve
half their maximal effect,

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:12.360
whereas the same is achieved
after just three weeks with estrogen.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.320
Of course, soy has the benefit

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:17.080
of no increased risk
of cancer and clots.

00:06:17.080 --> 00:06:19.680
In fact, if anything,
a reduction in breast cancer risk

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:24.040
throughout the lifespan, and lower
risk of cardiovascular disease.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:27.320
The bottom line, wrote one
consensus panel of experts,

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.240
is that soy can be considered
a first-line treatment

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.040
for menopausal hot flash
and night sweat symptoms.

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:36.560
One convenient whole food source
of soy is soy nuts,

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:38.680
which are dry roasted soybeans.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:41.440
Harvard Medical School’s Center
of Excellence in Women’s Health

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:47.160
funded a randomized crossover study
of a half cup of unsalted soy nuts a day

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.480
(divided into three or four portions
and spaced throughout the day)

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:55.640
and achieved a 50% reduction
in hot flashes within two weeks.

00:06:55.640 --> 00:06:57.840
What’s inconvenient about soy nuts

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:02.200
is the formation of advanced glycation
end products in the roasting process,

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:05.560
though about four times less than
that of roasted real nuts,

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:08.840
or other roasted legumes,
and ten times less than a serving

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:10.320
of something like roasted chicken,

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.960
but something like edamame
or canned soybeans would be better.

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:17.600
Some menopausal women
use isoflavone supplements,

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.480
but most just add soy foods
to their diet.

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:22.440
I think that’s the way to go.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:26.400
Supplement mislabeling
and contamination issues aside,

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.520
though isolated isoflavones have been
shown to improve artery function

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:32.280
and decrease high blood pressure.

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.880
Soy foods can also
lower LDL cholesterol

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:39.880
which may explain the lower associated
cardiovascular disease risk.

00:07:39.880 --> 00:07:43.240
Besides the lower breast cancer risk,
soy eaters are also less likely

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:47.927
to get prostate cancer,
colorectal cancer, and lung cancer.

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:51.800
What if you combined
a plant-based diet and soybeans?

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:54.440
Two randomized controlled trials
found that reduced-fat

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.880
plant-based diets
with a daily half cup serving

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.480
of cooked whole soybeans
can reduce the number

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:04.920
of serious hot flashes
by 84 to 88% within twelve weeks.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:07.440
Overall, most randomized
to the plant-based group

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:10.080
ended up free of moderate-
to-severe hot flashes,

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:15.918
compared to about 95% still suffering
in the control group.

